<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">French protectorate of Tonkin</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1945 CE · capital Hanoi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northern Vietnam under French rule from 1884, the most populous part of Indochina and the heartland of the Vietnamese independence movement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French expeditions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-asia">Red River</a> delta in the 1870s and 1880s, against Vietnamese forces and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag</a> irregulars, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hue-1884">Patenotre Treaty</a> of 1884, which made northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>, Bac Ky, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> of 1884 to 1885 ended Qing claims to the region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nominally under the Nguyen emperor, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trinh-lords">Tonkin</a> was in practice run directly by a French résident supérieur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, which became the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina</a> in 1902. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-bien-bridge">Long Bien bridge</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> railway and mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hon-gai">Hon Gai</a>, but heavy taxes, monopolies on salt and alcohol, and labour recruitment caused hardship. The delta was the main base of nationalist and communist organisations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the region from 1940 and ended French rule in March 1945. A famine in 1944 to 1945 killed perhaps a million people. In August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-protectorate-of-tonkin.jpg" alt="French protectorate of Tonkin" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: noUSERname · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colonial_Annam.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanoi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Paul Bert</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tonkin, Protectorate of Tonkin, Bắc Kỳ</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1914&amp;focus=french-protectorate-of-tonkin" aria-label="French protectorate of Tonkin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7526 1400 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French protectorate of Tonkin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7846 1656L7930 1822M7846 1656L5054 728M7846 1656L7939 2031"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7930 1822h0M5054 728h0M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hanoi. <a href="/atlas?year=1914&amp;focus=french-protectorate-of-tonkin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

